const REVIEW_COMMON = `You are an adversarial code reviewer for the zz TUI parity campaign (repo demfabris/zz). A worker just pushed a campaign branch; your verdict decides what the integration gate trusts. Read-only toward history: NEVER push, NEVER commit, NEVER touch the board or GitHub issues, never edit ${M.root} itself. Other lanes and reviews run beside you; keep to the parallelism caps.
HOW YOU REPORT: your final act is the structured report the output schema describes. FOREGROUND ONLY: run every command in the foreground (a plain Bash call with a timeout of at most 590000; split long batches); never use Monitor, run_in_background or any background task; never end your turn to wait. A reviewer that ends its turn without the report is a failed agent and the gate audits the lane itself.
SETUP: GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 git -C ${M.root} fetch origin +refs/heads/main:refs/remotes/origin/main '+refs/heads/campaign/*:refs/remotes/origin/campaign/*' (${M.gitNote}). Resolve the tip: git -C ${M.root} rev-parse origin/BRANCH. Scratch worktree at that tip: REVIEWDIR exists at origin/main and is clean, so checkout --detach the tip there (if it is dirty, git -C ${M.root} worktree add ${M.dev}/REVIEWDIR-2 <tip>). For every cargo command export CARGO_TARGET_DIR=WORKERTARGET, the worker's warm target directory (the worker is finished, its tree is clean at the same tip; the OTHER lanes' targets are NOT yours); touch crates/**/*.rs and Cargo.* in your worktree first so the shared directory rebuilds your tree, never cargo clean it, and rebuild before you spawn any binary from it. The shared checkout is ${M.root}; read it, never edit it. ${M.boxNote}
MACHINE ETIQUETTE: cargo test -p <pkg> --jobs ${M.workerJobs} -- --test-threads=${M.workerThreads} only, no workspace-scale anything, cargo output to a log file + check exit code. Throwaway pin servers -L zzprobe-$$ -f /dev/null only, kill after; never kill servers you did not start (${M.protected}), never pkill tmux or zz; never a bare tmux or zz command without -L.
METHOD, in order of value:
1. CONTRACT AUDIT: for every obligation the worker moved to review (worker report + git diff origin/main...HEAD -- compat/tui/campaign.json), take each acceptance clause and find the evidence on the branch that proves it against the PIN by an ASSERTED case. A recorded case inside a clause keeps that clause open whatever its reason string says, except a SIBLING:<lane> case that the batch assigns to that sibling; an accepted gap is never evidence. A fixture that only passes is not evidence: the sabotage runs must exist and must fail for the right reason. A clause claimed from a file that describes a run rather than records one is a blocker. The worker may have written status, evidence_note, next_action and sources of ITS obligation only; a proof block, a verified status, an edit to a verified record, to another lane's obligation, to the baseline, the milestones or the pin is a must-fix or blocker. ${OWNERSHIP}
2. PROOFS AT TIP: run the worker's claimed proof commands yourself at the branch tip with a zz you built in your worktree, with the pin ${PIN}. If the diff touches crates: the touched crates' tests and cargo test -p zz --jobs ${M.workerJobs} -- --test-threads=${M.workerThreads} (split across calls if it needs more than one). Any red at tip is a blocker regardless of the report (load-flake rule applies; the known list is in the batch prompt below).
3. ORACLE SPOT-CHECKS on the pin yourself: for every behaviour the lane changed or asserted, drive the PIN in a throwaway server and confirm the branch's fixture asserts what the pin actually does (read the pinned C where the batch names it). For every PRODUCT DECISION recorded, confirm the sentence, the old behaviour and the measured pin behaviour are present and true. Check every wait in new fixture code is a bounded wait_for; grep for sleep. Check the fixture reaps what it starts: run it, then pgrep -fa for its sockets and scratch HOME.
4. TEST HONESTY: run every new or flipped case's sabotage yourself; ADD one sabotage of your own on a throwaway copy and confirm the fixture reports it with a location; confirm evidence files are real captures from runs (sizes, contents), no .log files committed, git check-ignore -v flags nothing under the evidence directory, environment.txt matches a build you can reproduce from the recorded revision (hash identifies, revision attests).
5. INVARIANTS: zone discipline (git diff --stat origin/main...HEAD against the lane's declared zones; anything outside is a blocker unless notes declare it and the batch allows it); the wire rule (pure appends only, PROTOCOL_VERSION exactly 101 or untouched, consumer half in the same push, the copy lane touches no wire message; read the wire diff hunk by hunk); the GUI still builds (cargo build -p zz covers it) with no presentation change; no code comments added (grep the diff for added // lines outside fixture headers); no attribution trailers; both trackers' check green on the branch; generated reports regenerated, not hand-edited (regenerate and diff); gap items closed only inside the lane's named gaps and only for behaviour the diff makes the raw TUI honour.
6. CALIBRATION: default to refuting each claim, but confirmed_defects only with PROOF (probe, failing rerun, quoted contradiction). Suspicion goes in notes. blocker = a claimed clause without real evidence, a fixture that cannot fail, a zone or wire violation, or something that would break main; must-fix = gate applies before merge; nit = mention. The load-flake rule binds you too.
VERDICT: approve / approve-with-fixes / reject (a blocker the gate cannot fix in minutes; a rejected lane gets ONE fix pass by a fresh agent and a re-review, so make every blocker precise: the probe, the expected bytes, the file). Fill every_clause_asserted honestly: the gate will not promote an obligation you mark no. checks_run lists exact commands. Thorough but bounded: well under 90 minutes after the build.
`

const REVIEW_MODES = `LANE-SPECIFIC SPOT-CHECKS (modes): drive the pin's copy mode, a selection, display-message, command-prompt and run-shell output yourself and confirm tui-indicators.sh asserts the pin's cells for each, including the position indicator's text and style and the selection's last cell; confirm the raw TUI's status row shows no COPY badge; confirm the view surface is the pin's view-mode grid (position indicator, copy tables, q exits) and not a zz variant; read the StatusLine append hunk by hunk (pure append, per client, PROTOCOL_VERSION 101); confirm the gap items closed are exactly the ones the raw TUI now honours; run the lane's throwaway copy-mode flip yourself. State in every_clause_asserted whether tui-indicators.sh has ZERO recorded cases: this record was held at review over one open clause in cycles 3 and 4.`
const REVIEW_COPY = `LANE-SPECIFIC SPOT-CHECKS (copy): drive five stock copy-mode sequences of your own on both sides (a half page, a whole page, a search landing, a vi rectangle past the end of the last line, a prefix key that a copy table also binds) and compare the view, the cursor and the paste-buffer bytes against the fixture's claims; read the pinned window-copy.c lines the batch cites and confirm each engine fix matches them; confirm $table-prefix-takes-precedence can now fail (sabotage it); confirm selection_active, rectangle_toggle and pane_search_string match the pin at the fixture's checkpoints; confirm the SIBLING:modes reasons are exactly the three the batch assigns and nothing else stays recorded; confirm no wire message changed.`
const REVIEW_CAPS = `LANE-SPECIFIC SPOT-CHECKS (caps): print \\e[31mX, \\e[38;5;42mY and \\e[38;2;1;2;3mZ in a pane on both sides and read the classes through the outer tmux at the tip; change a palette entry and compare; confirm the GUI's painting path resolves the new class to the same RGB it painted before (read the code and its tests); confirm the default-foreground decision is recorded with the pin's measured behaviour and that no fixture-reachable state now paints a ground the pin leaves default; run -2, -u and -T yourself and compare client_utf8, client_termfeatures and client_flags on both sides; confirm the extended-keys claim by capturing the attach stream (script(1)) under the fixture's TERM and a tmux-class TERM; run tui-caps.sh, interrupt one run with SIGINT, and pgrep for leftovers.`
const REVIEW_OVERLAYS = `LANE-SPECIFIC SPOT-CHECKS (overlays): drive display-menu, display-popup, confirm-before, command-prompt and display-panes yourself on the pin and compare against the fixture's assertions (placement at an odd size, a title, the border, the selected item's style); press a key while a surface covers a pane and confirm the fixture proves the pane received nothing; confirm SIBLING:modes is used only for prompt or message style; confirm the gap items closed are exactly those the raw TUI now honours; confirm TUI-007's status note says it verifies only beside TUI-004.`
const REVIEW_CHOOSERS = `LANE-SPECIFIC SPOT-CHECKS (choosers): drive choose-tree (with a tag, a filter and the preview), choose-buffer and find-window yourself on the pin and compare against the fixture's assertions; if the preview landed, compare its cells at two sizes; if it did not, confirm the record names it open with the exact need; confirm SIBLING:modes is used only for command-output cases; confirm the GUI chooser is untouched in behaviour; confirm TUI-006's status note says it verifies only beside TUI-004.`

const LOCK = 'F-TUI-CYCLE-5-LANES'
const LANES = [
  { key: 'modes', prompt: BATCH_MODES, lock: LOCK, workdir: 'zz-tui-modes', reviewdir: 'zz-tui-modes-review', target: `${M.dev}/zz-tui-modes/target`, extra: REVIEW_MODES },
  { key: 'copy', prompt: BATCH_COPY, lock: LOCK, workdir: 'zz-tui-copy', reviewdir: 'zz-tui-copy-review', target: `${M.dev}/zz-tui-copy/target`, extra: REVIEW_COPY },
  { key: 'caps', prompt: BATCH_CAPS, lock: LOCK, workdir: 'zz-tui-caps', reviewdir: 'zz-tui-caps-review', target: `${M.dev}/zz-tui-caps/target`, extra: REVIEW_CAPS },
  { key: 'overlays', prompt: BATCH_OVERLAYS, lock: LOCK, workdir: 'zz-tui-overlays', reviewdir: 'zz-tui-overlays-review', target: `${M.dev}/zz-tui-overlays/target`, extra: REVIEW_OVERLAYS },
  { key: 'choosers', prompt: BATCH_CHOOSERS, lock: LOCK, workdir: 'zz-tui-choosers', reviewdir: 'zz-tui-choosers-review', target: `${M.dev}/zz-tui-choosers/target`, extra: REVIEW_CHOOSERS },
]
const OPTS = { model: 'opus', effort: 'xhigh' }

function reviewPrompt(r, rereview) {
  const head = rereview
    ? `RE-REVIEW after a reject: the first review's confirmed defects were:
${JSON.stringify(r.firstReview.confirmed_defects, null, 2)}
A fresh agent fixed the branch in place and pushed; verify each blocker is actually fixed at the NEW tip (re-run the first review's failing probes), then run the full method again on the whole branch.
`
    : ''
  return REVIEW_COMMON + '\n' + r.lane.extra + `
${head}LANE: ${r.lane.key}. BRANCH: ${r.worker.branch}. REVIEWDIR: ${r.lane.reviewdir}. WORKERTARGET: ${r.lane.target}.
THE LANE'S BATCH PROMPT (its zones, budgets and decisions; verify against it):
${r.lane.prompt}
WORKER REPORT (verify, do not trust):
${JSON.stringify(r.worker, null, 2)}`
}

function fixPrompt(r) {
  const defects = r.review.confirmed_defects || []
  const blockers = defects.filter(d => d.severity === 'blocker')
  const others = defects.filter(d => d.severity !== 'blocker')
  return `The adversarial review of the branch ${r.worker.branch} came back REJECT. You are a fresh agent taking over that lane: read the branch first (cd ${M.dev}/${r.lane.workdir}; git status --short; git log --oneline origin/main..HEAD; git diff origin/main...HEAD) and the review below before touching anything. The worktree ${M.dev}/${r.lane.workdir} is at the branch tip with a warm target; if git status --short is not empty that is the previous worker's uncommitted residue: inspect it, keep what belongs to the fix, discard nothing blindly. Fix every BLOCKER and every must-fix below on the SAME branch, push it (never force), and emit the FULL report again through structured output: every obligation of the original batch (carrying the original entries over, statuses corrected), sibling_cases, every proof command re-run at the NEW tip, the new tip sha in notes, and what each blocker's fix was. HARD BUDGET for this whole pass: 120 minutes plus the proofs. Prefer fixing the code to dropping the claim; if a blocker is a clause claimed without evidence and the fix does not fit, drop the claim: move the clause to clauses_open, set the obligation's status honestly, and put the reviewer's measurement into evidence_note.
BLOCKERS:
${JSON.stringify(blockers, null, 2)}
MUST-FIX AND NITS (apply the must-fixes too):
${JSON.stringify(others, null, 2)}
REVIEW NOTES: ${r.review.notes}
EVERY CLAUSE ASSERTED (reviewer): ${r.review.every_clause_asserted}
CHECKS THE REVIEWER RAN: ${JSON.stringify(r.review.checks_run)}
ORIGINAL WORKER REPORT:
${JSON.stringify(r.worker, null, 2)}
THE ORIGINAL BATCH PROMPT AND ITS RULES FOLLOW; they still bind you (same worktree, same branch, no new worktree):
${r.lane.prompt}`
}

log(`TUI cycle 5: ${LANE_KEYS}; Opus 5 at xhigh throughout, one reviewer per lane, one fix pass on a reject, one serialized gate with sibling flips`)
const laneResults = await pipeline(
  LANES,
  lane => agent(lane.prompt, { label: `worker:${lane.key}`, phase: 'Work', schema: WORKER_SCHEMA, ...OPTS })
    .then(w => ({ lane, worker: w })),
  r => {
    if (!r || !r.worker || !r.worker.branch) return r
    return agent(reviewPrompt(r, false), { label: `review:${r.lane.key}`, phase: 'Review', schema: REVIEW_SCHEMA, ...OPTS })
      .then(review => ({ ...r, review }))
  },
  r => {
    if (!r || !r.review || r.review.verdict !== 'reject') return r
    log(`${r.lane.key}: review REJECT, running one fix pass`)
    return agent(fixPrompt(r), { label: `fix:${r.lane.key}`, phase: 'Review', schema: WORKER_SCHEMA, ...OPTS })
      .then(fix => ({ ...r, firstWorker: r.worker, firstReview: r.review, worker: fix && fix.branch ? fix : r.worker, fixed: !!(fix && fix.branch) }))
  },
  r => {
    if (!r || !r.fixed) return r
    return agent(reviewPrompt(r, true), { label: `rereview:${r.lane.key}`, phase: 'Review', schema: REVIEW_SCHEMA, ...OPTS })
      .then(review => ({ ...r, review }))
  },
)
const summaries = laneResults.filter(Boolean).filter(r => r.worker && r.worker.branch)
  .map(r => ({ key: r.lane.key, lock_front: r.lane.lock, review: r.review || null, first_review: r.firstReview || null, fixed_after_reject: !!r.fixed, ...r.worker }))
log(`Lanes complete: ${summaries.length}/${LANES.length} branches pushed and reviewed. Integrating.`)
phase('Integrate')

const gatePrompt = `You are the integration gate for the zz TUI parity campaign (repo demfabris/zz, board = GitHub issue 7). The workers and reviewers are done; you run ALONE on this ${M.machine}, full speed. FOREGROUND ONLY: run every command in the foreground (a plain Bash call with a timeout of at most 590000; a single call is capped at 600 seconds and a longer one is moved to the background and killed, so split long batches into several calls: cargo test by package groups, the corpus by explicit scenario names); never use Monitor, run_in_background or any background task, and never end your turn to wait for one; you are done only when you have emitted the structured report. Up to five branches this cycle, serialized through ONE gate worktree. The summaries below carry each lane's worker report and review verdict; a lane may have been rejected once and fixed on the same branch, in which case review is the re-review and first_review the original.
Lane summaries, worker reports + Opus 5 review verdicts:
${JSON.stringify(summaries, null, 2)}
REVIEW VERDICTS BIND YOU: approve-with-fixes => apply every must-fix on that branch (own follow-up commit) before its gate, re-running the reviewer's failing probe to prove each fix. reject => do NOT merge that branch; post the blockers as a board note on the lock front, push the rebased tip as campaign/<name>-gated, leave the branch, and continue with the other lanes. A blocker you can genuinely fix in minutes may be fixed and merged with the probe re-run as proof; a clause claimed without evidence is such a fix (drop the claim, set the status honestly, put the measurement in evidence_note). review_actions must account for every confirmed defect. Missing review (null) => do a compressed contract audit yourself before gating that lane.
VERIFIED MEANS EVERY CLAUSE. Cycle 3's gate set TUI-004 verified while its own evidence_note said clause 3 was open; cycle 4's gate held it, correctly, because clause 3's cells sat in recorded cases. Before you set ANY obligation verified: read its acceptance list and its evidence_note at your tip and the reviewer's every_clause_asserted; any clause stated open or partial, or holding a recorded case (a SIBLING case you did not flip green included), keeps the obligation at the lane's honest status and the records commit says why. An accepted gap is never evidence. There is no partial verified. TUI-006 and TUI-007 depend on TUI-004: they can be set verified only in the same records commit that sets TUI-004 verified (the tracker checks dependencies).
SIBLING FLIPS: after each merge, for every merged lane's sibling_cases entry naming the lane just merged (and for any case whose reason string starts with 'SIBLING:<that lane key> '), flip the case from recorded to asserted in its fixture, in one commit per fixture ("Assert the cases the <lane> landing fixed"), and run that fixture plus its --self-check at the accumulated tip. Keep the flip only if both exit 0; otherwise revert that case's flip, keep it recorded with the failing channel appended to its reason, and the owning obligation stays out of verified. The modes lane committed a throwaway-flip run of compat/tui-copy-mode.sh as evidence; use it to predict, never as the proof. Report every flip in sibling_flips.
WIRE FOLD: every lane that appended to the wire took PROTOCOL_VERSION 100 to 101. When a later lane's rebase conflicts on the version constant or its tests, resolve to 101 (never 102), and after the last merge leave ONE v101 entry in knowledge/protocol/wire-protocol.md's version history (and its two managed mirrors, knowledge/protocol/index.md and knowledge/index.md) listing every append of the cycle, with the page's live version references reading 101. clients/web is an excluded workspace that references PROTOCOL_VERSION: say in problems whether you rebuilt it (just web-build) or could not.
BOARD IDENTITY: ZZ_BOARD_HOLDER=${M.holder} python3 compat/board.py <cmd> from inside a repo checkout. Verbs: release/withdraw REQUIRE --reason; note takes --note; candidate takes --commit --branch --base + repeatable --proof; integrated takes --merge + optional --gate; renew <FRONT> --lease 2h (always give a unit); withdraw and front need TRIAGE held; integrated needs MAIN held. The orchestrator holds the single lock front ${LOCK} covering all five lanes (expired => claim it back as ${M.holder} before the ledger step). BOARD FALLBACK: if a board command fails on GitHub authentication, append the exact command line to ${M.root}/compat/tui/board-replay-5.sh (do not commit that file), say so in board_updates, and continue; the orchestrator replays the file. Never let a board failure stop a merge.
${M.gitNote} Fetch git fetch origin +refs/heads/main:refs/remotes/origin/main '+refs/heads/campaign/*:refs/remotes/origin/campaign/*'; push git push origin HEAD:main. The shared checkout ${M.root} is where you start; its local main branch is stale; never use it, never edit it, never stash or reset in it. Every commit is git -c commit.gpgsign=false commit.
knowledge/tmux/tui-parity.md and knowledge/tmux/gaps.md are generated: regenerate with the trackers' write-report on every conflict, never hand-merge them. compat/tui/campaign.json and compat/tmux-gaps.json merge by record id: on a conflict, take each lane's changes to the records it OWNS and nothing else; in compat/tmux-gaps.json two lanes may close different items of one gap, so merge item by item. crates/zz-tui/src/render.rs, state.rs and input.rs conflicts resolve by union of both lanes' hunks; rebuild and run the fixtures to prove the union. crates/zz-mux/src/command.rs TMUX_OPTION_CONSUMERS and compat_manifest_tests.rs counts resolve by union with the counts recomputed.
THIS BOX: ${M.boxNote} Use ONE build directory for the gate worktree, export CARGO_TARGET_DIR=${M.dev}/zz-gate-target in every shell that runs cargo, compat/check.sh included (warm; leave it in place). Before each branch's gate, run git merge-tree --write-tree <current base> <tip>; it predicts the conflicts and costs one command.
ORDER: gate the branches in this order where present: modes (TUI-004, the wire's first append, and the target of most SIBLING cases), copy, caps, overlays, choosers. Later branches rebase onto the accumulating result.
STAGES, per branch, in the gate worktree ${M.root}-gate-tui5 (create once: git -C ${M.root} worktree add ${M.root}-gate-tui5 origin/main; remove a leftover with --force first). Claim MAIN --lease 6h before the first branch; renew before any long stage; release after the records push.
1. Rebase the branch onto the current gate HEAD (origin/main for the first, the accumulated result after). A conflict inside files the lane does not own that union cannot settle is a skip (push campaign/<name>-gated, record, continue).
2. Per branch: cargo test -p <every package the lane touched> and cargo test -p zz (the cli_binary integration tests) --jobs ${M.gateJobs} -- --test-threads=${M.gateThreads} > log 2>&1 (check the exit code; never pipe through tail); cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features --jobs ${M.gateJobs} -- -D warnings (it proves every crate, the GUI included, still compiles against the merged types); then ${RUN_ENV} compat/run.sh --strict-geometry --delta <base>..HEAD --commands <that lane's touched_commands> plus every keys and status scenario and smoke/tui-client-input-backpressure, split into calls that fit the cap (do not run corpus chunks in parallel: cycle 4's 6-way parallel run manufactured two load flakes). Flake rule: fails loaded + passes exact-solo = flake (the known list is in the batch prompts); anything else red is real: fix if minutes, else SKIP the branch (no merge; push the gated tip as campaign/<name>-gated), record, continue with the next branch from the last good HEAD.
3. Fixtures at the accumulated tip after each merge, then the SIBLING FLIPS for that merge: build zz (cargo build -p zz --jobs ${M.gateJobs}) and run with the pin ${PIN}: ${RUN_ENV} compat/tui-screen-diff.sh and its --self-check, compat/tui-pane-geometry.sh, compat/tui-stock-keys.sh, compat/status-row.sh (LC_ALL=C LC_TIME=C), compat/tui-indicators.sh, compat/tui-copy-mode.sh, compat/tui-caps.sh, and each merged lane's new fixture (tui-overlays.sh, tui-choosers.sh) with its --self-check. At the FINAL tip: every fixture plus every --self-check, compat/status-row.sh once under the box locale (the %b record), compat/attached-client.sh, and ONCE the full workspace suite (cargo test --workspace --all-features --no-fail-fast --jobs ${M.gateJobs} -- --test-threads=${M.gateThreads}, split by package groups across calls if one call cannot hold it) plus clippy. A red in the final workspace run that is not a load flake: find the owning merge by testing at the recorded merge points, back that branch out (push it as campaign/<name>-gated), and re-run. Then python3 -B compat/tui/tracker_test.py, python3 compat/tui/tracker.py check, python3 compat/tmux-tracker.py check, and compat/check.sh. After the gate, pgrep -fa 'zz-user|zzprobe' and reap anything the fixtures leaked (your own scratch HOMEs and sockets only).
4. RECORDS, after the last branch, one commit at the final tip: for each obligation whose lane merged with verdict approve or approve-with-fixes (fixes applied), whose reviewer marked every_clause_asserted yes (SIBLING cases aside) AND whose SIBLING cases you flipped green: cite stage 3's runs at this tip, write compat/tui/evidence/<ID>/<attempt>/review.md carrying the reviewer's verdict JSON verbatim, its checks_run, your review_actions and the sibling flips that touched it, and fill the proof block: revision = the final pre-records tip, tmux_commit = the campaign pin, environment = one line plus a pointer to environment.txt, commands = the exact invocations with exit codes, artifacts = the evidence files, review = the review.md path; set status verified and next_action to what its closure unlocks (TUI-005 and TUI-007 feed TUI-008; TUI-006 and TUI-007 feed TUI-011; TUI-009 feeds TUI-012, which also waits on TUI-008). Every other obligation stays at the lane's honest status with the reason appended to evidence_note. Say in problems exactly what the v101 wire carries. Then python3 compat/tui/tracker.py write-report, python3 compat/tmux-tracker.py write-report (if gaps changed), both checks, commit.
5. Push main (git push origin HEAD:main). Non-fast-forward: fetch; user-authored commits with a conflict-free disjoint rebase => bounded rerun (stage 3's fixtures at the new tip), push. Never force. Campaign branches stay at their old tips on origin (never force them); say so in the report.
6. Ledger against the single lock front ${LOCK}, once, after all branches: one candidate per merged branch posted on that front (--commit <its rebased tip> --branch <its campaign branch> --base <pre-push main> --proof per stage), then one note covering every lane (--note: obligations verified, left open and why, each reviewer verdict and what you did about each defect, the sibling flips; a skipped lane's blockers included), one integrated (--merge <the pushed sha> --gate "per-branch tests+clippy+delta corpus, sibling flips, fixtures, final workspace suite green"), one release (--reason "cycle 5 integrated at <sha>"). Release MAIN.
7. Claim TRIAGE. Post one residual listing what this cycle leaves: obligations left open with why, the divergences still without owners, the ready set at the pushed main, anything skipped, each with the obligation id. Release TRIAGE.
8. python3 compat/tui/tracker.py ready and the report headline (the Fixed baseline line of knowledge/tmux/tui-parity.md) at the pushed main, into progress_after.
9. Remove only your own ${M.root}-gate-tui5 worktree (after pushing any skipped lane's gated tip). Leave the lane and review worktrees in place. Sweep pgrep -fl zzprobe and reap only pids whose command line names a socket the gate created.
Never stash/reset anything in ${M.root}. Never kill tmux or zz servers you did not start (${M.protected}). Report per branch merged/sha/gate_summary/review_actions/flakes, the sibling flips, the progress lines, board records, the fixture outcomes, problems.`

const gate = summaries.length
  ? await agent(gatePrompt, { label: 'gate:serial', phase: 'Integrate', schema: GATE_SCHEMA, ...OPTS })
  : null
if (!gate) log('No branch to integrate: every worker came back empty.')
return { lanes: laneResults, gate }
